Write the event to the [[g:same database in the same transaction]] as the business data, then use a separate relay to publish it to the broker.

<pre><code>@Transactional
void placeOrder(Order order) {
    orderRepository.save(order);
    outboxRepository.save(new OutboxEvent("OrderCreated", order.getId(), toJson(order), PENDING));
    // commit together or roll back together — atomic because both writes use the same DB
}</code></pre>

- [[b:Polling]]: `SELECT ... WHERE status='PENDING' FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets multiple relay instances run in parallel without contending for the same rows. It is simple and often sufficient
- [[b:CDC]]: Debezium reads the transaction log (binlog/WAL) and publishes changes to Kafka. It offers low latency, adds no polling-query load, and preserves ordering, at the cost of more infrastructure to operate

:::deep
`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` lets N relays scan the same table while skipping rows another relay has locked → horizontal scaling without double-publishing through concurrent selection.

The Outbox pattern [[o:guarantees at-least-once, not exactly-once]] delivery. A crash before marking an event SENT causes it to be sent again, so the consumer [[r:must be idempotent]] (ch.09).
:::

The broker reports that it has "received the message and taken responsibility for it" with `basic.ack`.
- mode 1 confirms each message synchronously: safest, but slowest
- mode 2 confirms a batch: a nack does not identify which message failed, so resend the entire batch
- mode 3 handles confirms asynchronously: fastest

<pre><code>channel.confirmSelect();
ConcurrentNavigableMap&lt;Long, Message&gt; outstanding = new ConcurrentSkipListMap&lt;&gt;();
channel.addConfirmListener(
    (seqNo, multiple) -&gt; { if (multiple) outstanding.headMap(seqNo, true).clear();
                           else outstanding.remove(seqNo); },      // ACK
    (seqNo, multiple) -&gt; { /* NACK — resend or log+alert */ });
long seq = channel.getNextPublishSeqNo();
outstanding.put(seq, message);
channel.basicPublish(...);</code></pre>

Use `ConcurrentSkipListMap` because `multiple=true` is a cumulative acknowledgment covering every seqNo ≤ the reported value. `headMap(seqNo, true)` removes the acknowledged prefix in O(log n); the map remains safe under concurrency.

:::deep
Publisher confirms [[r:do not replace the Outbox pattern]]: they protect the app→broker hop, but they cannot help if the process crashes *after the database commit and before publish*, because the message was never sent.

Two traps:
- a confirm does not imply persistence; use `deliveryMode=2` and a durable queue
- asynchronous confirms [[r:do not resend automatically]] — your code must handle nacks and any messages left in `outstanding` when the channel dies
:::

The client sends an [[g:idempotency key]], typically a UUID representing one unique operation intent:

<pre><code>@PostMapping("/transfers")
Response transfer(@RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String key, @RequestBody TransferRequest req) {
    boolean firstTime = idempotencyStore.tryInsert(key, IN_PROGRESS);   // atomic
    if (!firstTime) {
        var rec = idempotencyStore.get(key);
        if (rec.status == COMPLETED)   return rec.savedResponse;         // return the original result
        if (rec.status == IN_PROGRESS) return Response.status(409);      // still processing
    }
    Response resp = doTransfer(req);
    idempotencyStore.update(key, COMPLETED, resp);                       // store the ENTIRE response
    return resp;
}</code></pre>

- [[o:Store the entire response]] because a retry needs the original transaction ID, not merely an empty 200 OK
- use an [[o:atomic CAS]] such as `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` backed by a unique constraint, never SELECT-then-INSERT, which races
- handle [[b:IN_PROGRESS]] by returning 409 so the client waits. Keep the TTL long enough to cover the legitimate retry window

:::deep
[[r:The trap]]: `doTransfer` completes, but the process crashes before `update` → the key remains IN_PROGRESS forever.

Put a timeout on IN_PROGRESS and make `doTransfer` idempotent at the next layer; ideally, include both operations in the same database transaction. Also use a [[g:natural key]] such as transferId as a database unique constraint → two independent safety nets.
:::
